  "activity": {
    "id": "act_vsepr_theory",
    "name": "VSEPR Theory and Molecular Geometry Lab",
    "description": "An interactive undergraduate chemistry lab covering VSEPR theory. Learners will progress from basic Lewis structures to 3D electron-domain and molecular geometries, observing the physical effects of lone pair repulsion on bond angles in molecules like CH4, H2O, SF6, and XeF4.",
    "language": "en",
    "workplace": "wp_vsepr_lab",
    "start": "act_intro_ch4",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "act_intro_ch4",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "ar_headset",
        "location": "p_theory_station",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Step 1: Steric Numbers and Lewis Structures",
          "description": "Welcome to VSEPR Theory. We will begin by looking at Methane (CH4) at the Theory Station. Examine its Lewis structure. By counting the electron domains around the central Carbon atom (four single bonds), we determine it has a Steric Number of 4. Click 'Next' to see how these domains arrange themselves in 3D space."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "p_theory_station",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_lewis_ch4"
            },
            {
              "target": "p_theory_station",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_label_steric_4",
              "poi": "top_display",
              "text": "Steric Number: 4"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "learner",
              "type": "action",
              "augmentation": "act_tetrahedral"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "p_theory_station",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "act_intro_ch4",
            "type": "action",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "act_tetrahedral",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "ar_headset",
        "location": "p_assembly_station",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Step 2: Electron-Domain Geometry",
          "description": "At the Assembly Station, we have a central atom. The fundamental premise of VSEPR is that electron domains repel each other to minimize repulsion. Say 'Repel' to watch the 4 bonding pairs of CH4 push apart into a Tetrahedral electron-domain geometry, settling at idealized bond angles of 109.5\u00b0."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "t_central_atom",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_tetrahedral_anim"
            },
            {
              "target": "t_central_atom",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_angle_109",
              "poi": "top_display",
              "text": "109.5\u00b0 Bond Angle"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "learner",
              "type": "action",
              "augmentation": "act_intro_h2o"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "t_central_atom",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "voice",
            "id": "act_tetrahedral",
            "type": "action",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "act_intro_h2o",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "ar_headset",
        "location": "p_theory_station",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Step 3: Introducing Lone Pairs",
          "description": "Return to the Theory Station. Now examine the Lewis structure for Water (H2O). The central Oxygen also has a Steric Number of 4, but the domains are mixed: 2 bonding pairs and 2 lone (non-bonding) pairs. Click 'Next' to see how lone pairs affect the shape."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "p_theory_station",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_lewis_h2o"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "learner",
              "type": "action",
              "augmentation": "act_bent"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "p_theory_station",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "act_intro_h2o",
            "type": "action",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "act_bent",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "ar_headset",
        "location": "p_assembly_station",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Step 4: Molecular vs Domain Geometry",
          "description": "Look at the central atom again. The electron-domain geometry is still tetrahedral, but lone pairs occupy more angular space than bonding pairs. This greater repulsion compresses the bonding pairs together. Observe the resulting 'Bent' molecular geometry and the reduced 104.5\u00b0 bond angle. Bring the Domain Tool near the atom to proceed."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "t_central_atom",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_bent_anim"
            },
            {
              "target": "t_central_atom",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_angle_104",
              "poi": "top_display",
              "text": "104.5\u00b0 Bond Angle (Compressed)"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "learner",
              "type": "action",
              "augmentation": "act_expanded_octets"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "t_central_atom",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "detect",
            "id": "t_domain_tool",
            "type": "tangible",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "act_expanded_octets",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "ar_headset",
        "location": "p_assembly_station",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Step 5: Complex Geometries and Expanded Octets",
          "description": "Move to the Molecule Stand. VSEPR also applies to expanded octets (Steric Numbers 5 and 6). Compare Sulfur Hexafluoride (SF6, 6 bonds, Octahedral) and Xenon Tetrafluoride (XeF4, 4 bonds + 2 lone pairs). Notice how the lone pairs in XeF4 position themselves 180\u00b0 apart (axially) to minimize mutual repulsion, yielding a Square Planar molecular geometry. Click to finish the lab."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "t_molecule_stand",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_octahedral_compare",
              "poi": "top_display"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "t_molecule_stand",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "act_expanded_octets",
            "type": "action",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
